---
/*  What the path encoder adds to the window every plugin shares: its path, a custom path's nodes, where the path
    sits, how the track travels it, and what it draws on the scene. The pictures are the path encoder's own, drawn
    by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['trajectory', 'trajectory'],
  ['custom', 'a custom path'],
  ['transform', 'transform'],
  ['source', 'source'],
  ['scene', 'the 3D view'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="path-encoder"
  sub="a source that moves along a path on the sphere, driven by its own audio"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('encoder-window'), alt: 'A path encoder window: five tracks as tabs, the 3D view with the paths, the matrix with three cells set, and the trajectory tab.', caption: 'a path encoder window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      The path encoder encodes a mono or stereo track into ambisonics and moves it along a path you draw on the
      sphere. Its speed can follow the input: silence stops the source, loud material moves it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has; the 3D view, the matrix, regions, presets and the link are
      explained in <a href={window}>the window</a>. This page covers what is the path encoder's own: its three tabs,
      <code>trajectory</code>, <code>transform</code> and <code>source</code>, and what it shows in the 3D view.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="trajectory" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-trajectory')} alt="The trajectory tab: lissajous chosen, its az and el amount, az and el ratio, phase, and convert to custom." width="696" height="700" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>trajectory</h2>
      <p>
        The path's shape. The row at the top chooses a shape, and under it are that shape's settings:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>orbit</code></strong>: <code>tilt</code>, <code>axis az</code> and <code>aperture</code>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>lissajous</code></strong>: <code>az amount</code> and <code>el amount</code>, how far it swings
          each way; <code>az ratio</code> and <code>el ratio</code>, how many times; and <code>phase</code>.
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>wave</code></strong>: <code>turns</code>, <code>el amount</code>, <code>wobbles</code> and <code>el offset</code>.</li>
        <li><strong><code>polygon</code></strong>: <code>sides</code>, <code>radius</code> and <code>rotation</code>.</li>
        <li><strong><code>spiral</code></strong>: <code>turns</code>, <code>from el</code>, <code>to el</code> and <code>start az</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The angles can be automated and modulated, so a path can move with the music. The whole numbers —
        ratios, turns, wobbles, sides — jump rather than sweep, so they are saved with the instance but not automatable.
        Choosing another shape resets its whole numbers to their defaults; its angles stay as you last set them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="custom" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-custom')} alt="The trajectory tab on a custom path: closed or open, node 3 of 8, smooth or corner, delete and make start." width="696" height="484" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>a custom path</h2>
      <p>
        <code>convert to custom</code> turns the path into nodes you edit on the sphere. The shape you converted
        from is kept: choose it again and its settings come back as they were. <code>custom</code> is offered once
        you have converted, and converting again replaces the custom path you have.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drag a node</strong> to move it, and its handles to bend the curve. One drag is one undo step.</li>
        <li><strong>Alt-drag a handle</strong> to make a corner.</li>
        <li><strong>Click the curve</strong> to add a node there.</li>
        <li><strong>Shift-click a node</strong> to delete it. A path keeps at least two nodes, and holds up to 24.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The tab holds the same, in words:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>closed</code></strong> or <strong><code>open</code></strong>: whether the path joins up.</li>
        <li>For the selected node: <strong><code>smooth</code></strong> or <strong><code>corner</code></strong>, and <strong><code>delete</code></strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>make start</code></strong>, on a closed path, makes the selected node the first: where the
          path is cut when it is opened.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <figure>
    <img src={img('encoder-nodes')} alt="The 3D view with a custom path: its nodes as white circles, the chosen one with its handles." width="1236" height="602" />
  </figure>

  <section id="transform" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('encoder-transform')} alt="The transform tab: yaw, pitch and roll, each with a rate, and extent." width="696" height="640" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>transform</h2>
      <p>Where the path sits and how it rotates, whatever its shape:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>yaw</code></strong>, <strong><code>pitch</code></strong> and <strong><code>roll</code></strong>,
          each beside a <strong>rate</strong> in degrees per second that keeps it rotating.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>extent</code></strong>, from 0 to 2: at 1 the path is as drawn, at 0 it shrinks to its own
          centre, and at 2 it flips out through the far side.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="source" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('values')} alt="The source tab: sum, mid/side and stereo; spread, offset and input trim; motion; render." width="696" height="1116" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>source</h2>
      <p>How the track sits on the path and moves along it, in three groups.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The input.</strong> <code>sum</code> puts one point on the path. <code>mid/side</code> puts the
          mid on the path and the side either side of it, <code>spread</code> apart. <code>stereo</code> puts left
          and right on the path, <code>offset</code> apart. <code>input trim</code>, ±24 dB, sets the level before
          it is analysed for modulation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>motion</strong>. <code>speed</code> is how fast the source moves, in degrees per second, and
          <code>displace</code> is where on the path it sits. <code>direction</code> is forward or reverse, and
          <code>mode</code> says what happens at the ends: <code>wrap</code>, <code>ping-pong</code> or
          <code>once</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>render</strong>. <code>width</code>, from a point to 180°, is how far the source spreads over
          the sphere, and <code>width range</code> is the range modulation can move it within.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the 3D view</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('encoder-scene')} alt="The 3D view: the selected track's path highlighted with its source on it, the other tracks' paths beside it." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The path is highlighted on the globe and the equirect, with the source on it coloured by its input
        signal. Every other path encoder in the session is drawn fainter beside it, so you can place each track
        against the others. <strong>Click another track's source or path</strong> to switch the window to that instance, as its tab in
        the header does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The kick nudges on every hit.</strong> Choose <code>polygon</code>, click <code>displace</code>, click
          the cell under <code>attack</code>, and drag it up.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The pad opens up when it rings.</strong> Click <code>width</code>, click the cell under <code>tonal</code>, and drag.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>It drifts slowly, and faster when it is loud.</strong> Set <code>speed</code> to a small value,
          then click <code>speed</code> and give it depth under <code>level</code>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
